Lab 9: Managing Virtual Machine Templates
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

• Create custom VM templates using YAML configuration files for standardized virtual machine deployment • Provision virtual machines from pre-defined templates using OpenShift Virtualization • Modify and customize templates to accommodate different VM configurations and requirements • Understand template inheritance and how to create variations for different use cases • Implement best practices for VM template management in enterprise environments

Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

• Basic understanding of YAML syntax and structure • Familiarity with Linux command line operations • Knowledge of virtual machine concepts such as CPU, memory, and storage allocation • Basic understanding of Kubernetes resources and object definitions • Access to OpenShift cluster with virtualization capabilities enabled

Required Knowledge Areas
• Understanding of virtualization fundamentals • Basic familiarity with container orchestration concepts • Knowledge of text editors like vi/vim or nano • Understanding of networking basics for VM connectivity

Lab Environment Setup
Al Nafi Cloud Environment: This lab uses Al Nafi's pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines. Simply click Start Lab to access your ready-to-use environment with OpenShift Virtualization already installed and configured. No additional VM setup required.

Environment Details
• Operating System: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 • OpenShift Version: 4.12 or later • Virtualization Operator: Pre-installed and configured • Storage: Dynamic provisioning enabled • Network: Default pod network configured

Task 1: Create a Custom VM Template Using YAML
Subtask 1.1: Understanding VM Template Structure
Before creating templates, let's understand the basic structure of a VM template in OpenShift Virtualization.

Key Components of a VM Template:

• Template metadata - Name, labels, and annotations • VM specification - CPU, memory, and disk requirements • Operating system information - Boot source and OS type • Network configuration - Network interfaces and connectivity • Storage configuration - Disk types and sizes

Subtask 1.2: Create the Base Template Directory
First, create a dedicated directory for your VM templates:

mkdir -p ~/vm-templates
cd ~/vm-templates
Subtask 1.3: Create a Basic Ubuntu VM Template
Create your first VM template for Ubuntu virtual machines:

cat > ubuntu-basic-template.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: template.openshift.io/v1
kind: Template
metadata:
  name: ubuntu-basic-vm-template
  namespace: default
  labels:
    template.kubevirt.io/type: vm
    os.template.kubevirt.io/ubuntu20.04: "true"
  annotations:
    name.os.template.kubevirt.io/ubuntu20.04: Ubuntu 20.04 LTS
    description: Template for creating Ubuntu 20.04 virtual machines
    tags: ubuntu,vm,linux
objects:
- apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
  kind: VirtualMachine
  metadata:
    name: ${VM_NAME}
    namespace: ${NAMESPACE}
    labels:
      app: ${VM_NAME}
      os.template.kubevirt.io/ubuntu20.04: "true"
  spec:
    running: false
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          kubevirt.io/domain: ${VM_NAME}
      spec:
        domain:
          cpu:
            cores: ${{CPU_CORES}}
          memory:
            guest: ${MEMORY}
          devices:
            disks:
            - name: rootdisk
              disk:
                bus: virtio
            - name: cloudinitdisk
              disk:
                bus: virtio
            interfaces:
            - name: default
              masquerade: {}
            networkInterfaceMultiqueue: true
            rng: {}
        networks:
        - name: default
          pod: {}
        volumes:
        - name: rootdisk
          containerDisk:
            image: quay.io/kubevirt/ubuntu-container-disk:20.04
        - name: cloudinitdisk
          cloudInitNoCloud:
            userData: |
              #cloud-config
              user: ubuntu
              password: ${USER_PASSWORD}
              chpasswd: { expire: False }
              ssh_authorized_keys:
                - ${SSH_PUBLIC_KEY}
              package_update: true
              packages:
                - qemu-guest-agent
              runcmd:
                - systemctl enable qemu-guest-agent
                - systemctl start qemu-guest-agent
parameters:
- name: VM_NAME
  description: Name of the Virtual Machine
  required: true
- name: NAMESPACE
  description: Namespace where VM will be created
  value: default
- name: CPU_CORES
  description: Number of CPU cores
  value: "2"
- name: MEMORY
  description: Amount of memory
  value: 2Gi
- name: USER_PASSWORD
  description: Password for ubuntu user
  generate: expression
  from: '[a-zA-Z0-9]{12}'
- name: SSH_PUBLIC_KEY
  description: SSH public key for ubuntu user
  value: ""
EOF
Subtask 1.4: Create an Advanced CentOS Template
Now create a more advanced template for CentOS with additional customization options:

cat > centos-advanced-template.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: template.openshift.io/v1
kind: Template
metadata:
  name: centos-advanced-vm-template
  namespace: default
  labels:
    template.kubevirt.io/type: vm
    os.template.kubevirt.io/centos8: "true"
  annotations:
    name.os.template.kubevirt.io/centos8: CentOS Stream 8
    description: Advanced template for CentOS Stream 8 virtual machines
    tags: centos,vm,linux,advanced
objects:
- apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
  kind: VirtualMachine
  metadata:
    name: ${VM_NAME}
    namespace: ${NAMESPACE}
    labels:
      app: ${VM_NAME}
      environment: ${ENVIRONMENT}
      os.template.kubevirt.io/centos8: "true"
  spec:
    running: ${{START_VM}}
    template:
      metadata:
        labels:
          kubevirt.io/domain: ${VM_NAME}
      spec:
        domain:
          cpu:
            cores: ${{CPU_CORES}}
            threads: ${{CPU_THREADS}}
          memory:
            guest: ${MEMORY}
          devices:
            disks:
            - name: rootdisk
              disk:
                bus: virtio
            - name: datadisk
              disk:
                bus: virtio
            - name: cloudinitdisk
              disk:
                bus: virtio
            interfaces:
            - name: default
              masquerade: {}
            networkInterfaceMultiqueue: true
            rng: {}
        networks:
        - name: default
          pod: {}
        volumes:
        - name: rootdisk
          containerDisk:
            image: quay.io/kubevirt/centos-container-disk:8-stream
        - name: datadisk
          emptyDisk:
            capacity: ${DATA_DISK_SIZE}
        - name: cloudinitdisk
          cloudInitNoCloud:
            userData: |
              #cloud-config
              user: centos
              password: ${USER_PASSWORD}
              chpasswd: { expire: False }
              ssh_authorized_keys:
                - ${SSH_PUBLIC_KEY}
              package_update: true
              packages:
                - qemu-guest-agent
                - htop
                - git
                - curl
                - wget
              write_files:
              - path: /etc/motd
                content: |
                  Welcome to ${VM_NAME}
                  Environment: ${ENVIRONMENT}
                  Created from template: centos-advanced-vm-template
              runcmd:
                - systemctl enable qemu-guest-agent
                - systemctl start qemu-guest-agent
                - mkfs.ext4 /dev/vdb
                - mkdir -p /data
                - mount /dev/vdb /data
                - echo '/dev/vdb /data ext4 defaults 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
parameters:
- name: VM_NAME
  description: Name of the Virtual Machine
  required: true
- name: NAMESPACE
  description: Namespace where VM will be created
  value: default
- name: ENVIRONMENT
  description: Environment label (dev/test/prod)
  value: dev
- name: CPU_CORES
  description: Number of CPU cores
  value: "2"
- name: CPU_THREADS
  description: Number of CPU threads per core
  value: "1"
- name: MEMORY
  description: Amount of memory
  value: 4Gi
- name: DATA_DISK_SIZE
  description: Size of additional data disk
  value: 10Gi
- name: START_VM
  description: Start VM after creation
  value: "false"
- name: USER_PASSWORD
  description: Password for centos user
  generate: expression
  from: '[a-zA-Z0-9]{16}'
- name: SSH_PUBLIC_KEY
  description: SSH public key for centos user
  value: ""
EOF
